if the sirens weren't sounding already  when it comes to strikes they almost  certainly will be now ambulance work is  the first of the emergency services to  announce walkouts this winter with two  strikes planned either side of Christmas  affecting non-life-threatening calls  only  Simon's a paramedic and will join a  picket line in the West Midlands in 14  years in the job he says the pressures  have never been so great it's very much  like you've driven your ambulance into a  tunnel and there's no light at the end  of it the the the level of hopelessness  and the decrease in morale of staff  is just something that I have not seen  in my experience and many people who  have been in for a lot longer than I  have  haven't seen either like other disputes  this is about pay the three main  ambulance unions are demanding above  inflation pay hikes planning action that  affects all but one trust in England and  Wales Trade union rules mean that life  preserving care must still be provided  so things like Strokes heart attacks and  major trauma will still be responded to  but the exact details of this so-called  life and limb cover are yet to be  decided and it could mean that trips and  falls for example don't Merit a call out  for the elderly or for those who require  care it is a worrying Prospect Lisa  Roland suffered multiple strokes and  heart attacks in recent years but still  supports the strikes I don't know what  I'd do if it happened and I couldn't get  an ambulance that's scary but what can  we do I mean they need to do something  they don't get enough money I mean it's  ridiculous so even though you're worried  you still support their cause yeah  absolutely but instead of a countdown to  Christmas this is now a December of  disruption with industrial action of  some kind planned for almost every day  this month unions say it was a last  resort what people don't realize is that  the NHS is already crumbling beneath us  it's been a political decision to  deliberately underfund and  under-resource the NHS for the last  decade and one of the reasons our  members are taking this action is  because nobody is listening  the government's proposed a four percent  payoffer with health secretary Steve  Barkley calling the union demands  unaffordable all signs will be hoping  it's not patience paying the price  either Bennett Sky News
 

